    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d748">

                <pb facs="1489" n="1449" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg1449"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">684A.85/12–253: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 748<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Jordan (Mallory) to the
                        Department of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d748-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Amman</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-12-02T18:00:00+02:00">December 2, 1953—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1253. Deptel 231.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d748-f2"/> Jordan Government today
                    informed Arab diplomatic representatives it proposed reply to invitation UNSYG
                    to conference with Israel as follows:</p>
                <p>Jordan Government will tell SYG proposed meeting impracticable this time. Jordan
                    prepared to discuss with Israel any time within framework mixed armistice
                    commission any amendments desired by Israel or claims she may have or clarify
                    ambiguities. All this can be done, according to Jordan, within framework of MAC
                    under Article II mixed armistice agreement.</p>
                <p>Jordan Government asked immediate comment other Arab Governments on proposed
                    attitude.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Comment:</hi> This tentative decision due intransigence .
                    . . section government led by Foreign Minister Khalidy. Believed decision will
                    be disapproved by many responsible Jordanians including several members cabinet.
                    Decision taken despite repeated strong representations by US and British urging
                    acceptance UNSYG’s invitation. Today UK Ambassador again urged Prime Minister
                    accept and Lynch again spoke emphatically at Foreign Office.</p>
                <p>Approval by other Arab Leaguers acceptance of invitation might change local
                    attitude but we not assured even this would overcome intransigence and fear.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mallory</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d748-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d748-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Paris, London, Baghdad, Beirut, Cairo, Damascus, Jidda, Tel Aviv, and
                    Jerusalem; repeated by the Department to New York.</p>
            </note>
